<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">What Is the Third Estate?</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Qu'est-ce que le Tiers-État?</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">What Is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a>? is a political pamphlet by the French writer and clergyman Abbé <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Emmanuel Joseph Sieyès</a>, written between 6 November and 12 December 1788 and published in January 1789, shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. It answered finance minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-necker">Jacques Necker</a>'s invitation to say how the Estates-General should be organised. Sieyès argued that the third estate, the common people, formed a complete nation in itself, that the clergy and nobility were unnecessary, and that the nation was sovereign; he asked for representation equal to the other two orders together and votes by head. Some 300,000 copies were printed, and it made Sieyès one of the leaders of the Estates-General.</p></div>
